{
  "requirement": "Each public SDK item has one defining module path. The SDK exposes no public\nre-exports. The chapter that introduces each supported type or free function owns its declaration.\nThe [public API inventory](sdk/public-api.md) links each item to that requirement,\nwhich describes its purpose\nand declares its public Rust interface, including fields, enum variants, trait\nmembers, and inherent methods. A change to that interface requires a change to\nthe owning requirement. Every supported struct with public fields is\n`#[non_exhaustive]`. Its constructor accepts the values needed to create it.\nAdditional fields have consuming builder methods that accept a concrete value.\nOptional fields start as `None`. Backend inference options start empty and can\nbe added one key at a time.\n\nThe SDK’s public modules follow the interfaces introduced in these chapters.\n\n- `domain`: application data and the `Domain` and `NodeContents` traits.\n- `graph`: criterion definitions, graph observations, evidence, and functions for running criteria.\n- `request`: `ValidationContext`, typed `Ask` builders, context values, and request phases.\n- `agent`: configuration for the supplied agents.\n- `sources`: project inspection, search queries, and their results.\n- `outline`: selectable source contents and language rules; `outline::languages`\n  supplies bundled languages, and `outline::selector` defines selector syntax.\n- `storage`: filesystem evidence storage.\n\nSource access, search engines, redaction, provider transports, agent-result paging,\nand conversation machinery are implementation details of those interfaces.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately cover `requirements.sdk.public_api`.\n\n**Obligations and the tests that check them**\n\n- **No public re-exports.** `tests/public_api.rs#fn[sdk_has_no_public_reexports]` parses every `.rs` file under `src` with tree-sitter. It fails on any `use_declaration` with a plain `pub` visibility modifier, and it also fails if a file cannot be parsed.\n- **One defining module path, with every public item inventoried.** `fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` runs `cargo doc` for this source tree and walks the rustdoc item tables from the crate root through nested modules. It compares the resulting set of (path, kind) pairs with the inventory parsed by `specified_exports`. It asserts that nothing is missing or unexpected, and a duplicate export panics. Rustdoc lists re-exports as extra entries, so any alternate path to an item would show up as an unexpected export.\n- **The inventory links each item to its owning requirement.** `specified_exports` and `declaration_target` require a declaration link for every non-module item. The link must point to a narrative chapter and not to `public-api.md`, and its anchor must be a valid `habeas-req-requirements.sdk.*` anchor. `check_declaration` then requires the owning requirement to exist, to have descriptive prose and a ```rust,ignore``` declaration, and to link its members.\n- **The interface is declared exactly.** `check_declaration` compares the requirement's declared fields, variants, trait members and inherent methods with rustdoc's item declaration. It checks inherent members, trait implementations, and member names, and fails on both missing and unexpected entries. `fn[member_order_is_free_but_field_and_method_types_are_committed]` checks the comparison helpers directly. Reordering methods is accepted. Changing a field type, a return type, or `&mut self` to `&self` is rejected.\n- **A public-field struct is `#[non_exhaustive]`.** `check_declaration` asserts that any exported struct whose page has `structfield` entries has `#[non_exhaustive]` in its published declaration. This runs for every exported struct.\n- **Constructor, consuming builders, `None` defaults, and options that start empty and are added one key at a time.** These are covered at the behavior level by the tests marked for this requirement:\n  - `agent/config.rs#mod[tests]/fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]` builds each backend config from `new`. It serializes the result to show that optional fields are absent, which means `None`, and that options are absent or empty. It chains consuming builders and adds options one key at a time, including replacing a key.\n  - `outline_content.rs#fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` checks `OutlineItem::new` defaults and `Outline` diagnostic `None`.\n  - `source_defaults.rs` checks `SourceFile` language `None` and the `TextQuery` and `StructuralQuery` limit defaults of `None`. It also covers their builders.\n  - `outline/selector.rs#mod[tests]/fn[parses_named_anonymous_and_ordinal_segments]` covers `Segment` and `SelectorError` construction.\n- **Public modules follow the listed interfaces.** The module set is compared exactly against the inventory by the exports test.\n\n**Remaining observations**\n\nBuilder methods that accept a concrete value are enforced by the signature comparison against the declarations. They are also enforced by the tests' `\"..\".into()` calls, which would not compile against `impl Into` parameters.\n\nOther constructor tests, such as those for `Record`, `Snapshot`, `Fingerprint`, `SearchResults` and `OutlineId`, have no `public_api` marker. Those types have no builder-added optional fields, and their constructor signatures are already checked by the rustdoc declaration comparison. I treat this as no gap.\n\nNo {fixme} acknowledgments apply. I found no unaddressed MUST or SHOULD clause, so I report no test gap.",
  "references": "83e5a024def69d2c249a2488b0d6e064785b414d91c24616a0f007d173d67065",
